    },
    {
      "name": "failure_mode",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Failure mode is a genuine new axis for modeling resolution time. Grounded for cases like 'connection unstable' -> connection_failure, but 'Unable to access the database' -> connection_failure is a specific inference the text does not clearly support (could be permissions), indicating some overconfident labeling."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_scope",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Scope (individual vs office/department-wide) is a strong candidate driver of resolution time. However most short_descriptions are silent on scope, yet 280 rows are labeled 'individual_user' as a confident default — that asserts specifics the text does not state. Unknown values for silent rows would be correct; the default weakens groundedness."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_ticket_unresolved",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Largely derivable from state/closed_at and is nearly constant (478 false / 22 true), and partly redundant with the original state column, giving limited analytical leverage for comparing agent resolution times. Grounded where derived, but low-variance."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.72,
  "source_groundedness": 0.75,
  "rationale": "Most added columns provide genuine new grouping/modeling axes (category, severity, failure_mode, resolver_match, scope) directly relevant to explaining resolution-time differences, so table-level usefulness is solid; is_ticket_unresolved is near-constant and redundant. Groundedness is generally good for extractions from short_description and original fields, but several columns over-assert specifics the terse text does not support — notably affected_scope defaulting to 'individual_user' and failure_mode inferring connection_failure for access issues — pulling groundedness down from high."
